<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carl Benz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1844">1844</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Karl Benz, Karl Friedrich Benz, Karl Friedrich Michael Vaillant</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carl Benz was a German engine designer and automotive engineer whose Benz Patent-Motorwagen of 1885 is considered the first practical, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobile</a>. Born in Mühlburg, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a>, he studied at Karlsruhe's polytechnical school and founded workshops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a>, where he patented a two-stroke engine in 1880. He received a patent for the motorcar in 1886 and began selling it in 1888, the year his wife Bertha Benz drove one 104 km from Mannheim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pforzheim">Pforzheim</a>. His firm Benz &amp; Cie. merged with Daimler Motoren Gesellschaft in 1926 to form Daimler-Benz. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladenburg">Ladenburg</a> in 1929.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carl-benz.jpg" alt="Carl Benz" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (same photo credited to various stock photo agencies. The copyright is also claimed by the Mercedes-Benz Public Archive) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carl_Benz_circa_1920.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Carl Benz was born Karl Friedrich Michael Vaillant on 25 November 1844 in Mühlburg, in the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, to Josephine Vaillant and Johann Georg Benz, a locomotive driver, whom she married a few months later; the child then took the name Benz. His father died of pneumonia when he was two. His mother, living in near poverty, gave him a good education, and he passed the entrance examination for mechanical engineering at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a> polytechnical school on 30 September 1860, aged 15. He graduated on 9 July 1864.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1871 Benz joined August Ritter in founding an iron foundry and mechanical workshop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a>, and when it went badly his fiancée Bertha Ringer bought out Ritter's share with her dowry. They married on 20 July 1872. He finished a petrol two-stroke engine on 31 December 1879 and patented it on 28 June 1880. While perfecting it he patented the speed regulation system, battery spark ignition, the spark plug, the carburettor, the clutch, the gear shift and the water radiator. In 1883 he founded Benz &amp; Cie. with Max Rose and Friedrich Wilhelm Eßlinger.</p><p>Benz applied for a patent on his motorcar on 29 January 1886 and drove it publicly on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ringstrasse">Ringstrasse</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> on 3 July 1886. Patent number 37345 followed in November, and the vehicle became known as the Benz Patent-Motorwagen. He began selling it in the late summer of 1888. On 5 August 1888 Bertha Benz took one with her sons Eugen and Richard on a trip of 104 km from Mannheim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pforzheim">Pforzheim</a>, repairing faults on the way and having leather nailed to the brake blocks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Benz &amp; Cie. grew from 50 employees in 1889 to 430 in 1899, and in 1899 it produced 572 vehicles, making it the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobile</a> company in the world. Benz announced his retirement from design management on 24 January 1903 but stayed on the board. On 28 June 1926 the firm merged with Daimler Motoren Gesellschaft as Daimler-Benz, and he died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladenburg">Ladenburg</a> on 4 April 1929, aged 84.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carl Benz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
